    "content_html": "<div class=\"articlebodycontent col-xl-9 col-lg-12 col-md-12 col-sm-12 col-12\" id=\"content-body-71733919\"><div class=\"schemaDiv\" id=\"schemaDiv\"><p>Many years ago on a stifling hot day in July, I arrived at Phuphee’s house, exhausted in body and completely broken in spirit. We had just lost a family member, who had passed away after being diagnosed with cancer. We had spent the last year watching her change so drastically that, by the end, she no longer resembled the person she used to be. The memory of that time will haunt me for the rest of my days.</p><p>Right after she died, something else perished in my life without any warning. I had a friend whom I had believed myself to be close to. On the day of the death, my friend arrived, offered their condolences and left, never to be seen again. It was confusing and disorientating because the rhythms of our daily lives had, up until that point, been mildly dictated by each other. This was a death that had definitely not been foretold.</p></div><div class=\"articleblock-container\" id=\"arthardpv\"></div><div class=\"update-publish-time article-published-time-end\"><p class=\"publish-time-new\"> Published <span>- November 26, 2026 01:30 pm IST</span></p></div><div class=\"comments-shares share-page flex print-hide\" data-artid=\"71733919\" data-title=\"Column | The summer I learnt to replant\" data-url=\"https://www.thehindu.com/opinion/columns/column-saba-mahjoor-phuphee-life-lessons-growth-and-renewal/article71733919.ece\"><div class=\"left\"><div class=\"share\"><button class=\"btn share-btn\" data-ga-labelclick=\"ArticleShare\"></button><ul class=\"share-list bottom\" style=\"display: none;\"><li class=\"copy-link\" data-cf-modified-9edb0ee20bb43fdc934b7e59-=\"\" onclick=\"if (!window.__cfRLUnblockHandlers) return false; copytext('https://www.thehindu.com/opinion/columns/column-saba-mahjoor-phuphee-life-lessons-growth-and-renewal/article71733919.ece');\">\n                Copy link  \n    